Indian ocean, between the Malabar coast and the Red Sea. {{smaller|Large numbers of baggalas trade between Muscat, the Red Sea, and India, making one voyage each way annually with the monsoons. They are generally of from 200 to 250 tons burden, are exceedingly weatherly, and are remarkable for the elevation of the stern, which is highly ornamented. Also ''bagla'' and ''buggalow''.}}

'''bagge†''', ''v. i.'' [ME., found only twice, in the apparent sense of ‘squint,’ or ‘look aside’; adv. ''baggingly'', q. v. Origin obscure.] A word of doubtful meaning, probably, to squint or look aside.

{{smaller block|False fortune . . . that ''baggeth'' foule, and looketh faire.}}
